The Pakiad Illusion: Love, Leftovers, and Unrehearsed Vows

They say art imitates life, but on the dust-blown edge of the Circumferential Road, it just feels like cinema is mocking our grocery budget. Passing by the mini public market of Brgy. Pakiad, Oton, my eyes caught the pristine glare of the teaser poster for "A Perfect Marriage". Spotted under the low-slung, rustic roof of FQ Grill and Restaurant , it offered a masterclass in irony: two beautifully stylized lovers gazing into a cinematic forever, while a mere five feet away, a bold sign screamed "EAT AT YOUR OWN RISK. NO LEFTOVERS."

As a card-carrying member of the fandom, I couldn’t help but appreciate the poetic dissonance. Movies give us the immaculate, slow-burn tension directed by Dan Villegas. Reality, however, smells of freshly steamed talaba, unli-rice grease, and the frantic honking of tricycles cutting through the Pakiad crossing. A real-world marriage isn't a high-budget Metro Manila Film Festival drama; it’s an all-you-can-eat buffet. You dive in with grand expectations, you quickly realize you took on more than you could chew, and you are strictly forbidden from taking the leftovers home.

The poster makes love look linear, quiet, and perfectly lit. But looking at it from across the street, framed by plastic monobloc chairs and the colorful chaos of the local talipapa market, you realize the truth: the perfect marriage isn't found in the pristine script. It’s found in the messy, uncurated margins of everyday survival.

The Twist Ending:
Moved by the cinematic romance of the poster, I immediately texted my husband a picture of it, adding a soft, nostalgic caption: "This could be us. Let's start over, fix the cracks, and build our own perfect marriage."

He replied two minutes later, completely ignoring the text: "Basi gusto mo mag-unli talaba sa FQ Grill? 199 lang, ara pa ko Pakiad. Pero dalia kay mapuno na sila, strictly no sharing."

And just like that, the illusion shattered. I walked inside, grabbed a plate, and realized that maybe a perfect marriage isn’t about matching expectations—it’s about finding someone who will help you clear the buffet table before the 9:00 PM cut-off.

